      <h2>What Is Akari?</h2>
      <p><strong>Akari</strong> (あかり, meaning "light" in Japanese) is a logic puzzle created by <a href="https://www.nikoli.co.jp/en/">Nikoli</a>, the legendary Japanese puzzle publisher behind Sudoku, Kakuro, and dozens of other beloved deduction games. First published in 2001, the <strong>akari puzzle</strong> quickly became a favorite among fans of Japanese logic puzzles for its unique combination of spatial reasoning and constraint satisfaction.</p>
      <p>In an <strong>akari puzzle</strong>, you are given a rectangular grid containing black walls and white cells. Some black cells carry numbers from 0 to 4. Your task is to place light bulbs on white cells so that every white cell is illuminated — each bulb sends light in four directions (up, down, left, right) until it hits a wall. The numbered cells tell you exactly how many bulbs sit in their four adjacent positions. And the critical constraint: no two bulbs can see each other along the same row or column without a wall between them.</p>
      <p>Like Sudoku, Akari is a grid-based deduction puzzle with a unique solution — but instead of placing numbers, you manage lines of sight. This makes it a <strong>spatial logic puzzle</strong> rather than an arithmetic one, which is why many players find it more relaxing and intuitive than number-based puzzles. You can <strong>play akari online</strong> for free on GridPaw, where the classic puzzle is reimagined with adorable glowing cats instead of light bulbs.</p>

      <h2>The History of Akari</h2>
      <p>Nikoli has been inventing and publishing logic puzzles since 1980. Their editorial philosophy is strict: every puzzle must have a unique solution solvable through pure logic alone. No guessing, no trivia, no luck — just deduction. This philosophy applies to all Nikoli creations, and the <strong>akari puzzle</strong> is no exception.</p>
      <p>Akari joined Nikoli's roster of iconic puzzles alongside Sudoku (which Nikoli popularized worldwide in 2004), Kakuro (cross-sum puzzles), Slitherlink (loop-drawing), and Nonograms (pixel art by numbers). While Sudoku captured the global spotlight, Akari quietly built a devoted following among players who preferred spatial reasoning over number manipulation.</p>
      <p>The puzzle spread internationally through Nikoli's quarterly puzzle magazine, newspaper syndication, and eventually online puzzle sites. Today, you can find <strong>akari online</strong> on dozens of websites, but the puzzle's core remains unchanged from Nikoli's original 2001 design. The name "Light Up" is the most common English localization, though purists and Japanese puzzle fans still call it by its original name: Akari.</p>

      <h2>Complete Akari Rules</h2>
      <p>If you are new to the <strong>akari puzzle</strong>, here are the four rules that govern every puzzle:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Numbered black cells</strong> — A black cell with number N (0–4) must have exactly N light bulbs in its four orthogonally adjacent cells (up, down, left, right). A "0" means none of its neighbors can hold a bulb; a "4" means all four neighbors must be bulbs.</li>
        <li><strong>Illumination</strong> — Each light bulb illuminates its entire row and column until the light hits a black wall or the grid edge. Every white cell must be illuminated by at least one bulb.</li>
        <li><strong>No bulb conflicts</strong> — Two light bulbs cannot see each other in the same row or column without a wall between them. If you place two bulbs in an unobstructed line, the puzzle will flag the violation.</li>
        <li><strong>Complete illumination</strong> — The puzzle is solved when every white cell is illuminated and all numbered constraints are satisfied simultaneously.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>That is all there is to it. Four rules, infinite possibilities. The simplicity of the ruleset is what makes the <strong>akari puzzle</strong> so elegant — and so challenging on larger grids.</p>

      <h2>Akari vs Other Logic Puzzles</h2>
      <p>How does the <strong>akari puzzle</strong> stack up against other popular logic puzzles? Here is a comparison:</p>
