      <p>The <strong>akari puzzle</strong> stands out because of its real-time visual feedback. As you place bulbs, you can literally watch light spread across the grid. This makes progress feel tangible and satisfying in a way that number-placement puzzles cannot match. It is also one of the few logic puzzles where the core mechanic (illumination) is inherently visual rather than abstract.</p>

      <h2>Akari Solving Techniques</h2>
      <h3>Technique 1: Constraint Propagation</h3>
      <p>This is the foundation of every <strong>akari puzzle</strong> solve. Start by examining numbered black cells:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>A <strong>"4" cell</strong> — all four neighbors must be bulbs. Place them immediately.</li>
        <li>A <strong>"0" cell</strong> — none of its neighbors can be bulbs. Mark all four as impossible.</li>
        <li>A <strong>"3" cell</strong> with one neighbor already blocked — the remaining three must all be bulbs.</li>
        <li>A <strong>"2" cell</strong> with two bulbs already placed — mark remaining neighbors as impossible.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>After each placement, propagate the consequences: newly placed bulbs illuminate rows and columns, which may force or eliminate other placements. Repeat until no more simple deductions are available.</p>
      <h3>Technique 2: Line-of-Sight Elimination</h3>
      <p>When you place a bulb in an <strong>akari puzzle</strong>, it illuminates its entire row and column. This means:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>No other bulb can be placed in the same unobstructed line — doing so creates a conflict.</li>
        <li>Every cell in the illuminated line is "satisfied" — it does not need another bulb to light it.</li>
        <li>An unlit cell that can only be reached from <strong>one remaining direction</strong> forces a bulb in that direction.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This "last remaining option" technique is the most common way to break through a stuck position. Scan the grid for dark cells and count how many valid positions could illuminate them.</p>
      <h3>Technique 3: Elimination Reasoning (Advanced)</h3>
      <p>When constraint propagation and line-of-sight elimination are not enough, use hypothetical deduction. Pick an undecided cell and mentally assume "a bulb goes here." Propagate all consequences — illumination, neighbor counts, conflict zones. If you reach a contradiction (a numbered cell gets too many bulbs, or a white cell becomes impossible to illuminate), then your assumption was wrong and that cell cannot hold a bulb.</p>
      <p>This technique is what separates Easy <strong>akari puzzles</strong> from Hard ones. On a 12×12 grid with sparse clues, you may need to chain several hypothetical deductions before making progress. The key is to choose assumptions that affect the most cells — placing or eliminating a bulb near a cluster of numbered walls gives you the most information.</p>
      <h3>Technique 4: Shadow Mapping</h3>
      <p>Advanced solvers mentally overlay a "shadow map" on the grid. For each undecided cell, track which directions could still illuminate it. As you place bulbs and mark impossibilities, the shadow map narrows. When a cell's shadow map shows only one viable direction, that direction is forced. This systematic approach prevents you from overlooking subtle deductions on larger grids.</p>

