          <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #e7dacf;"><td style="padding:.4rem;"><strong>Medium</strong></td><td style="padding:.4rem;">10×10</td><td style="padding:.4rem;">Propagation + 1–4 elimination steps</td><td style="padding:.4rem;">Intermediate solvers</td></tr>
          <tr><td style="padding:.4rem;"><strong>Hard</strong></td><td style="padding:.4rem;">12×12</td><td style="padding:.4rem;">Propagation + multiple elimination chains</td><td style="padding:.4rem;">Experienced puzzlers</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>If you are new to <strong>akari online</strong>, start with Easy puzzles to build intuition. The 7×7 grid has more numbered clues relative to its size, which means more forced placements and fewer dead ends. Once you can solve Easy puzzles without hints, move to Medium. Hard puzzles are for players who have mastered all four techniques above.</p>

      <h2>Why GridPaw's Cat Theme Works</h2>
      <p>The original Akari uses light bulbs — functional but visually cold. GridPaw replaces them with <strong>glowing cats</strong>: warm, round, and emotionally inviting. This is not just decoration. The cat theme serves a practical purpose: it lowers the intimidation barrier for new players. A grid full of cute cats looks like play, not homework. The warm golden illumination creates a cozy atmosphere that makes solving feel meditative rather than stressful.</p>
      <p>The cat theme also adds a layer of emotional engagement. When you place a cat and watch its light spread across the grid, the visual feedback is more satisfying than a plain light bulb icon. This emotional connection keeps players coming back — which is why GridPaw has become a popular destination for <strong>akari online</strong> play.</p>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <details><summary>Is Akari the same as Light Up?</summary><p>Yes. "Akari" is the original Japanese name by <strong>Nikoli</strong>. "Light Up" is the English localization. Same puzzle, same rules, same logic. GridPaw uses both names.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is an akari puzzle different from Sudoku?</summary><p>Akari uses spatial reasoning (lines of sight, adjacency) instead of number placement. No math is needed — just careful observation and logical deduction. Both are Nikoli puzzles with unique solutions.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can every akari puzzle be solved without guessing?</summary><p>Yes, if it has a unique solution. All <strong>akari puzzles</strong> on GridPaw are verified to have exactly one solution reachable through pure logic. If you feel stuck, re-examine your numbered cells — there is always a deduction you missed.</p></details>
